Assistant: All examples match. The solution works by iterating through `[lower, upper]`, tracking the start of each consecutive missing run, and closing a range whenever a present number (or the end of the interval) is encountered. This runs in O(n + (upper - lower)) time, well within the constraints.

```python
class Solution:
    def findDisappearedNumbers(self, nums: list[int], lower: int, upper: int) -> list[list[int]]:
        present = set(nums)
        ranges = []
        start = None
        for x in range(lower, upper + 1):
            if x not in present:
                if start is None:
                    start = x
            else:
                if start is not None:
                    ranges.append([start, x - 1])
                    start = None
        if start is not None:
            ranges.append([start, upper])
        return ranges
```